<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr (Onze Jan)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Onze Jan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr, known as Onze Jan, was a Cape politician and journalist who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaner-bond">Afrikaner Bond</a> and was the most influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> politician in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s ministry until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895–1896 and campaigned for the recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. He is not to be confused with his nephew Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr, the later South African minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-hendrik-hofmeyr-onze-jan.jpg" alt="Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr (Onze Jan)" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OnzeJanHofmeyr_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hofmeyr was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> on 4 July 1845 and educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-cape-town">South African College</a>. He turned early to politics, first as a journalist: he edited de Zuid-Afrikaan until it was merged with Ons Land, and the Zuid Afrikaansche Tijdschrift. A Dutch Afrikaner by birth, education and sympathy, he set out to organise the political power of his fellow countrymen, and when he entered the Cape parliament for Stellenbosch in 1879 he became the real leader of the Dutch party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He held office only once, as minister without portfolio in the Scanlen ministry from May to November 1881, but his control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaner-bond">Afrikaner Bond</a> let him make and unmake ministers, which earned him the name Cabinet-maker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. His habit of working behind the scenes gave him another, the Mole. He represented the Cape at the intercolonial conference of 1887, where he moved a resolution for an imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a>, and at the Colonial Conference of 1894 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>. Though he defined the term Afrikander to include white men of any origin, the Bond's influence always served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><p>In the Bechuanaland crisis of 1884 he backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, but he drifted away from President Kruger, resenting his fiscal policy, fearing the Transvaal's new wealth after the gold discoveries of 1886 and holding the protection of the British navy indispensable. In 1890 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, who became premier with the Bond's support, and helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> convention of 1890. As the Bond's reactionaries grew alarmed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>'s imperialism, Hofmeyr gave up his seat and the Bond's presidency in 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He helped draft Sir Hercules Robinson's proclamation disavowing Jameson, then took the Bond's presidency again. He protested against abuses such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>'s dynamite monopoly and urged Kruger to make concessions rather than risk war; in July 1899 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> to support a fair franchise law and a limited representation of the Uitlanders in the Volksraad, in vain. In 1880 he married Aleda Hendrikz and in 1900, after her death, her sister Johanna. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 11 October 1909.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr (Onze Jan)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cape politics was shaped by the Afrikaner Bond under <b>Jan Hofmeyr</b> and by English-speaking mining interests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.93,18.42&amp;year=1886" aria-label="Cape Town on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jan Hendrik Hofmeyr (Onze Jan)"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4184 5377h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4184 5377h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.93,18.42&amp;year=1886">What was here in 1886? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
